  <a class="skip-link" href="#main-content">Skip to main content</a>

  <header class="page-header">
    <span class="page-header__eyebrow">The Cat Compendium</span>
    <h1>Frequently Asked Questions</h1>
    <p class="page-header__subtitle">Answers to life's most important questions — all about cats.</p>
  </header>

  <main id="main-content">

    <!-- ── Section 1: Behaviour ──────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="faq-section" aria-labelledby="section-behaviour">
      <h2 class="faq-section__label" id="section-behaviour">Behaviour &amp; Body Language</h2>
      <ul class="faq-list">

        <li>
          <div class="example">
            <h3>
              <button type="button" class="faq-trigger" aria-expanded="false">
                Why do cats purr?
                <!-- Chevron icon (decorative) -->
                <svg class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"
                     viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"
                     stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5"
                     stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
                  <polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/>
                </svg>
              </button>
            </h3>
            <div class="details">
              <p>Cats purr by rapidly dilating and constricting the glottis — the part of the larynx that surrounds their vocal cords — during both inhalation and exhalation.</p>
              <p>While purring is often a sign of contentment, cats also purr when stressed, injured, or even giving birth. Some researchers believe the 25–50 Hz vibration frequency may promote bone density and tissue healing.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </li>

        <li>
          <div class="example">
            <h3>
              <button type="button" class="faq-trigger" aria-expanded="false">
                What does it mean when a cat slow-blinks at you?
                <svg class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"
                     viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"
                     stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5"
                     stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
                  <polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/>
                </svg>
              </button>
            </h3>
            <div class="details">
              <p>A slow blink — where a cat half-closes its eyes and then reopens them — is a sign of trust and affection. It is often called the "cat kiss."</p>
              <p>Studies have shown that humans who slow-blink back at cats are more likely to receive a slow blink in return. Try it: make soft eye contact and blink slowly — it can build rapport with cats you have just met.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </li>

        <li>
          <div class="example">
            <h3>
              <button type="button" class="faq-trigger" aria-expanded="false">
                Why do cats knead with their paws?
                <svg class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"
                     viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"
                     stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5"
                     stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
                  <polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/>
                </svg>
              </button>
            </h3>
            <div class="details">
              <p>Kneading is a behaviour kittens use to stimulate milk flow from their mother. Many cats retain it into adulthood as a self-soothing reflex — you will often see it when a cat is relaxed or settling down to sleep.</p>
              <p>Cats also have scent glands in their paw pads, so kneading a surface or a person is a way of marking something as comforting and familiar.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </li>

        <li>
          <div class="example">
            <h3>
              <button type="button" class="faq-trigger" aria-expanded="false">
                Why do cats bring their owners "gifts" like prey?
                <svg class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"
                     viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"
                     stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5"
                     stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
                  <polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/>
                </svg>
              </button>
            </h3>
            <div class="details">
              <p>Cats are solitary hunters but social animals. The leading theory is that a cat presenting prey to you is treating you as part of its social group — essentially teaching you to hunt or sharing a resource, much as a mother cat would with her kittens.</p>
              <p>Indoor cats sometimes substitute toys for prey. Accepting the "gift" calmly and without punishment (even if it is a real mouse) preserves the trust your cat is extending to you.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </li>

      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Section 2: Health & Care ─────────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="faq-section" aria-labelledby="section-health">
      <h2 class="faq-section__label" id="section-health">Health &amp; Care</h2>
      <ul class="faq-list">

        <li>
          <div class="example">
            <h3>
              <button type="button" class="faq-trigger" aria-expanded="false">
                How often should I take my cat to the vet?
                <svg class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"
                     viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"
                     stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5"
                     stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
                  <polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/>
                </svg>
              </button>
            </h3>
            <div class="details">
              <p>Most veterinary associations recommend an annual wellness exam for healthy adult cats (ages 1–10). Senior cats — typically over 10 years old — benefit from twice-yearly check-ups because age-related conditions can progress quickly.</p>
              <p>Kittens need a series of vaccinations and check-ups during their first year. Between scheduled visits, watch for changes in appetite, water intake, litter box habits, or coat quality, which can be early warning signs.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </li>

        <li>
          <div class="example">
            <h3>
              <button type="button" class="faq-trigger" aria-expanded="false">
                Can cats eat a vegan diet?
                <svg class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"
                     viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"
                     stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5"
                     stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
                  <polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/>
                </svg>
              </button>
            </h3>
            <div class="details">
              <p>Cats are obligate carnivores, meaning their bodies require nutrients — such as taurine, arachidonic acid, and preformed vitamin A — that are found almost exclusively in animal tissue. They cannot synthesise these in sufficient quantities on their own.</p>
              <p>A diet without animal protein puts cats at serious risk of heart disease, vision loss, and neurological damage. If you are concerned about sustainability, look for brands that source protein from insects or laboratory-cultivated meat rather than eliminating animal protein entirely.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </li>

        <li>
          <div class="example">
            <h3>
              <button type="button" class="faq-trigger" aria-expanded="false">
                Why does my cat drink so little water?
                <svg class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"
                     viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"
                     stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5"
                     stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
                  <polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/>
                </svg>
              </button>
            </h3>
            <div class="details">
              <p>Cats evolved in arid environments and historically obtained most of their moisture from prey. Their thirst drive is naturally lower than a dog's or a human's, and they can concentrate urine efficiently — though this also makes them prone to kidney and urinary-tract issues if chronically under-hydrated.</p>
              <p>Feeding wet food, using a pet drinking fountain (flowing water triggers the drinking reflex), and placing multiple water stations away from the food bowl can all increase daily intake.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </li>

      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Section 3: Fun Facts ──────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="faq-section" aria-labelledby="section-facts">
      <h2 class="faq-section__label" id="section-facts">Fun Facts</h2>
      <ul class="faq-list">

        <li>
          <div class="example">
            <h3>
              <button type="button" class="faq-trigger" aria-expanded="false">
                Do cats always land on their feet?
                <svg class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"
                     viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"
                     stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5"
                     stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
                  <polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/>
                </svg>
              </button>
            </h3>
            <div class="details">
              <p>Cats have a highly developed "righting reflex" — a sequence of spinal and vestibular movements that lets them orient their body during a fall so their feet point downward. This reflex is fully developed by about 7 weeks of age.</p>
              <p>However, "always" is too strong. Falls from very low heights (under about 60 cm) do not give enough time for the reflex to complete, while falls from very great heights can still cause serious injury. Cats are not immune to fall trauma — never assume a fall is safe.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </li>

        <li>
          <div class="example">
            <h3>
              <button type="button" class="faq-trigger" aria-expanded="false">
                How many hours a day do cats sleep?
                <svg class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"
                     viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"
                     stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5"
                     stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
                  <polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/>
                </svg>
              </button>
            </h3>
            <div class="details">
              <p>Most adult cats sleep between 12 and 16 hours per day; older cats and kittens can sleep up to 20 hours. As crepuscular predators (most active at dawn and dusk), cats conserve energy during the day for short, intense bursts of activity.</p>
              <p>Much of a cat's daytime rest is light, drowsy sleep rather than deep REM sleep — which is why they often seem to spring into action from an apparently dead sleep in under a second.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </li>

      </ul>
    </section>

  </main>

  <footer class="page-footer">
    <p>The Cat Compendium &mdash; built with care for curious cat lovers everywhere.</p>
  </footer>

