  <a class="skip-link" href="#main-content">Skip to main content</a>

  <header class="page-header">
    <span class="page-header__eyebrow">The Cat Compendium</span>
    <h1>Frequently Asked Questions</h1>
    <p class="page-header__subtitle">Answers to life's most important questions — all about cats.</p>
  </header>

  <main id="main-content">

    <!-- ── Section 1: Behaviour ──────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="faq-section" aria-labelledby="section-behaviour">
      <h2 class="faq-section__label" id="section-behaviour">Behaviour &amp; Body Language</h2>
      <ul class="faq-list">

        <li>
          <div class="example">
            <h3>
              <button type="button" class="faq-trigger" aria-expanded="false">
                Why do cats purr?
                <!-- Chevron icon (decorative) -->
                <svg class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"
                     viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"
                     stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5"
                     stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
                  <polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/>
                </svg>
              </button>
            </h3>
            <div class="details">
              <p>Cats purr by rapidly dilating and constricting the glottis — the part of the larynx that surrounds their vocal cords — during both inhalation and exhalation.</p>
              <p>While purring is often a sign of contentment, cats also purr when stressed, injured, or even giving birth. Some researchers believe the 25–50 Hz vibration frequency may promote bone density and tissue healing.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </li>

        <li>
          <div class="example">
            <h3>
              <button type="button" class="faq-trigger" aria-expanded="false">
                What does it mean when a cat slow-blinks at you?
                <svg class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"
                     viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"
                     stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5"
                     stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
                  <polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/>
                </svg>
              </button>
            </h3>
            <div class="details">
              <p>A slow blink — where a cat half-closes its eyes and then reopens them — is a sign of trust and affection. It is often called the "cat kiss."</p>
              <p>Studies have shown that humans who slow-blink back at cats are more likely to receive a slow blink in return. Try it: make soft eye contact and blink slowly — it can build rapport with cats you have just met.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </li>

        <li>
          <div class="example">
            <h3>
              <button type="button" class="faq-trigger" aria-expanded="false">
                Why do cats knead with their paws?
                <svg class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"
                     viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"
                     stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5"
                     stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
                  <polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/>
                </svg>
              </button>
            </h3>
            <div class="details">
              <p>Kneading is a behaviour kittens use to stimulate milk flow from their mother. Many cats retain it into adulthood as a self-soothing reflex — you will often see it when a cat is relaxed or settling down to sleep.</p>
              <p>Cats also have scent glands in their paw pads, so kneading a surface or a person is a way of marking something as comforting and familiar.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </li>

        <li>
          <div class="example">
            <h3>
              <button type="button" class="faq-trigger" aria-expanded="false">
                Why do cats bring their owners "gifts" like prey?
                <svg class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"
                     viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"
                     stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5"
                     stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
                  <polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/>
                </svg>
              </button>
            </h3>
            <div class="details">
              <p>Cats are solitary hunters but social animals. The leading theory is that a cat presenting prey to you is treating you as part of its social group — essentially teaching you to hunt or sharing a resource, much as a mother cat would with her kittens.</p>
              <p>Indoor cats sometimes substitute toys for prey. Accepting the "gift" calmly and without punishment (even if it is a real mouse) preserves the trust your cat is extending to you.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </li>

      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Section 2: Health & Care ─────────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="faq-section" aria-labelledby="section-health">
      <h2 class="faq-section__label" id="section-health">Health &amp; Care</h2>
      <ul class="faq-list">

        <li>
          <div class="example">
            <h3>
              <button type="button" class="faq-trigger" aria-expanded="false">
